um_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"ETF shares outstanding change only in the **primary market**, where Authorized Participants (APs), typically large broker-dealers, deal directly with the fund.\n\n**Creations (shares increase):**\n- When demand pushes the ETF's price above NAV, an AP delivers a basket of underlying securities (or cash) to the fund.\n- The fund issues new shares in large blocks called **creation units** (often 10,000–50,000 shares).\n- The AP sells them on the exchange, adding supply and pulling the price toward NAV.\n\n**Redemptions (shares decrease):**\n- When the price falls below NAV, an AP buys ETF shares in the market and returns a creation unit to the fund.\n- The fund delivers the underlying basket (or cash) and **cancels** those shares.\n\n**Key point:** Secondary-market trading between investors doesn't change shares outstanding; only AP activity does. This arbitrage keeps prices near NAV, and in-kind redemptions support tax efficiency.","ttft_ms":1701,"type":"result","duration_ms":3920,"uuid":"79a153ca-5e20-4a9b-949b-7f24aaf6ec4b","ttft_stream_ms":1429,"time_to_request_ms":62,"first_content_frame_ms":1430,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
